Looking at this problem, I need to find the maximum product of all 20 distances between vertices of an inscribed square (radius 1) and an inscribed regular pentagon (radius 2), both concentric.

**Setup.** Place both circles at the origin. Square vertices at angles $\theta + 90k$ ($k=0,1,2,3$) on radius 1; pentagon vertices at angles $\phi + 72j$ ($j=0,1,2,3,4$) on radius 2. Let $\delta = \theta - \phi$.

The distance between a vertex at angle $\alpha$ (radius 1) and one at angle $\beta$ (radius 2) is
$$d = \sqrt{1+4-4\cos(\alpha-\beta)} = \sqrt{5-4\cos(\alpha-\beta)}.$$

**Product of 20 distances.** Let $P = \prod_{k,j}(5-4\cos(\delta+90k-72j))$, so the answer is $\sqrt{P}$.

**Pentagon step (fix $k$, set $\alpha=\delta+90k$).** Using the identity $\prod_{j=0}^{n-1}(t-\cos(\alpha+2\pi j/n)) = 2^{1-n}(T_n(t)-\cos n\alpha)$:
$$\prod_{j=0}^{4}(5-4\cos(\alpha+72j)) = 4^5\prod_j\!\left(\tfrac54-\cos(\alpha+72j)\right) = 4^5\cdot\tfrac{1}{16}\bigl(T_5(\tfrac54)-\cos 5\alpha\bigr).$$
With $T_5(x)=16x^5-20x^3+5x$ and $T_5(5/4)=3125/64-2500/64+400/64=1025/64$:
$$= 256\!\left(\tfrac{1025}{64}-\cos 5\alpha\right) = 4100 - 256\cos 5\alpha.$$

**Square step.** Now $\alpha = \delta+90k$, so $5\alpha = 5\delta+450k \equiv 5\delta+90k\pmod{360}$. Thus
$$P = \prod_{k=0}^{3}\bigl(4100-256\cos(5\delta+90k)\bigr) = 256^4\prod_k\!\left(\tfrac{4100}{256}-\cos(5\delta+90k)\right).$$
Using $n=4$: $\prod_k(\cdots)=\tfrac18\bigl(T_4(\tfrac{1025}{64})-\cos 20\delta\bigr)$, so
$$P = \tfrac{256^4}{8}\bigl(T_4(\tfrac{1025}{64})-\cos 20\delta\bigr).$$

**Maximize.** Since $\delta$ is a free relative rotation, $P$ is maximized when $\cos 20\delta = -1$ (achievable, e.g. $\delta=\pi/20$):
$$P_{\max} = \tfrac{256^4}{8}\bigl(T_4(\tfrac{1025}{64})+1\bigr).$$
Using $T_4(x)+1 = 8x^4-8x^2+2 = 2(2x^2-1)^2$, with $x=1025/64$:
$$2x^2-1 = \tfrac{2\cdot 1050625}{4096}-1 = \tfrac{2097154}{4096} = \tfrac{1048577}{2048} = \tfrac{2^{20}+1}{2^{11}}.$$
So $T_4(1025/64)+1 = 2\cdot(2^{20}+1)^2/2^{22} = (2^{20}+1)^2/2^{21}$.

Therefore
$$P_{\max} = \tfrac{256^4}{8}\cdot\tfrac{(2^{20}+1)^2}{2^{21}} = \tfrac{2^{32}}{2^3}\cdot\tfrac{(2^{20}+1)^2}{2^{21}} = \tfrac{2^{32}\cdot(2^{20}+1)^2}{2^{24}} = 2^8(2^{20}+1)^2.$$

The product of distances is $\sqrt{P_{\max}} = 2^4(2^{20}+1) = 16\cdot 1048577 = 16777232$.

$$\boxed{16777232}$$

